{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>My teaching shall drop as the rain.</b> The words that I say to you are not idle words, but just as rain falls and its benefit and goodness are not recognized or visible, yet in the end it grows fruits, so too <b>my teaching</b> and <b>my speech</b>. Another interpretation: it is an expression of prayer — may it be His will that my words not return empty, but produce fruit in the hearts of the listeners, like the deed of rain and dew upon the earth, to bring it to birth and make it sprout. He mentioned here the four winds of the world: just as he called heaven and earth to witness, so he called upon them the four winds of the world, that if they keep the Torah, good winds will blow for them." "<b>My teaching shall drop as the rain.</b> This is the west wind, which comes from the back of the world, and its way is to bring rain." "<b>Distil as the dew.</b> The north wind, which is gentle as dew." "<b>As the small rain.</b> The south wind, which pours down like showers." "<b>And as the showers.</b> The east wind, which multiplies seeds and grows plants. And they blow every day: the east wind in the morning, the south wind at midday, the west wind at the beginning of the night, and the north wind at midnight. In the summer the south wind is fine, and in the rainy season it is harsh — reversed; the west wind is always harsh, the east wind is always fine."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:55:01.482620169Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}